1184149597,"Jeremy Clarkson teases final episode of The Grand Tour with exciting behind-the-scenes snap
Jeremy Clarkson, James May and Richard Hammond will soon be reuniting for one last adventure as the trio's beloved Amazon Prime Video series The Grand Tour draws to a close
Jeremy Clarkson has set the internet alight, teasing fans with a snapshot from the final episode of The Grand Tour on X (formerly Twitter).
The hit Amazon Prime Video series, which follows the adventures of the ex-Top Gear presenters embarking on car-related challenges around the world, delighted viewers with another gripping adventure titled Sand Job, set in the vast Western Sahara and released back in February.
In November 2023, it was announced that Jeremy, 64, together with co-stars James May and Richard Hammond, had completed filming what is expected to be their last instalment of the beloved series.
Jeremy's latest post offers a sneak peek behind the scenes, showing an evocative image of the trio sitting atop a rock against a Zimbabwean desert backdrop.
The three hosts were kitted out for the harsh African climate, sporting sunglasses and matching brown boots, ready for whatever comes their way.
Jeremy captioned the photo simply: ""Filming the new GT #thegrandtour.""
The date of the photo is unknown, particularly as Jeremy has claimed to be in the Cotswolds this week on Instagram, hinting that there may not have been additional shoots in Zimbabwe.
This tantalising preview has stirred up excitement among die-hard fans, with many taking to social media to share their thoughtsone notably commented, ""The last episode of the trio [salute emoji].""
""Actually going to miss you 3 messing about in cars Sir..... Its been an absolute pleasure watching,"" another fan expressed.
A somewhat melancholic viewer responded: ""This is it guys, end of an era"" accompanied by a downcast emoji.
""Great news , great tv and all 3 back together. Can't wait,"" a fourth enthusiast chimed in.
Despite Jeremy, James and Richard leaving the series, The Grand Tour fans might have cause for optimism as rumours surfaced in April that the show could carry on with a fresh set of presenters.
Moreover, there's also a fourth season of Clarkson's Farm in the pipeline after the third instalment was launched in May.
