1316901622,"Ceuta migrant surge shines spotlight on Morocco-Spain tensions
Sara-Jayne Makwala King
5 August 2026 | 9:02
What was really behind the surge of thousands of migrants who attempted to enter Spain’s North African exclave of Ceuta?
Ceuta Spanish Morrocon border (Wikicommons)
They came in their tens of thousands. Migrants desperate to reach Europe.
Many swam around border barriers or squeezed through fences into the Spanish enclave of Ceuta from Morocco.
But the mass crossing left dozens dead and has raised fresh questions about the political tensions behind the surge.
Located at the tip of North Africa and bordering Morocco, Ceuta has been under Spanish control since the 1500s.
Morocco regards it as a lasting vestige of colonialism.
Both sides blame human traffickers for smuggling migrants, but questions are also being raised about whether there is a geopolitical invisible hand behind the mass migration.
Migration expert Dr Loren B. Landau told 702 that most of those attempting the crossing appeared to be Moroccan citizens, with many driven by poverty and the hope of finding better economic opportunities in Europe.
“It’s definitely not something that happened spontaneously where people just woke up that morning and in their thousands decided to cross the border.”
ALSO READ: 34 people died trying to cross from Morocco into Spain's Ceuta enclave: official
Landau agreed that there appears to be a political element to the surge, but said the exact motivations remained unclear.
""What it seems is that they were mobilised, if not by international traffickers, by the Moroccan government themselves, through public media, through other mechanisms as a kind of assault on Spain,"" he said.
He also noted the long-running dispute over Western Sahara, a former Spanish colony that Morocco claims as its own territory.
It's a region that has been contested for decades.
Landau said Morocco may have been using the migrant surge to put pressure on Spain to back its claim over Western Sahara.
""There has been an ongoing military battle with the Polisario Front in Western Sahara, and Spain has been ambiguous in endorsing Morocco's claims to that. This may be a way in which they can say, look, we will continue to assault you on this side if you will not support our claims for Western Sahara.""
Another possibility, he said, was that Morocco wanted more money from Spain in exchange for helping to control migration into Europe.
""They've looked at what Turkey has done and said, 'Look, if we're going to keep migrants out of Spain, you're going to have to pay us more. We can open this tap. We can make things difficult for you, so pay up.'""
