1230264124,"Rabat – Spain’s Foreign Minister José Manuel Albares has again voiced his country’s commitment to resolving the long-standing Western Sahara dispute while elevating Morocco’s critical and strategic importance as a key partner.
Making the statements in an interview with El Pais, the Spanish foreign minister unequivocally reiterated his country’s support for Morocco’s autonomy initiative, echoing a “broad consensus among European countries.”
Albares spoke of Spain’s primary objective to find a mutually agreeable solution between the two parties after 50 years of dispute.
“Morocco is a first-degree friendly and strategic partner for Spain and the European Union,” he said, pointing to the multifaceted nature of their relationship.
He recalled Morocco’s crucial role in combating human trafficking networks and terrorism, further solidifying its strategic significance.
The foreign minister also talked about the robust economic ties between the two countries. Current bilateral trade volumes are comparable only to those with the United States and the United Kingdom, demonstrating the depth of their economic connection.
In a significant diplomatic development, Albares referenced recent infrastructural changes, including the reopening of customs facilities in Melilla and the establishment of a customs office in Ceuta – a historic first.
Responding to questions about Spain’s stance, Albares reiterated the country’s support for the work of UN Secretary-General’s Personal Envoy Staffan de Mistura, expressing hope that a comprehensive solution can be proposed that addresses the concerns of all parties involved.
The minister’s statements reflect Spain’s commitment to maintaining a strong, strategic partnership with Morocco, viewing the country as a reliable ally in addressing major challenges such as migration and counterterrorism efforts.
Read also: Spain Officially Endorses Morocco’s Autonomy Plan for Western Sahara
A critical time
Spain’s show of support comes at a crucial time, following the European country’s recent diplomatic blunder. Reports recently surfaced of photos showing Spain’s King Felipe VI next to Polisario leader Brahim Ghali during the inauguration of Uruguay’s new leftist president last Saturday.
However, political pundits have asserted that these photos do not indicate that Spain’s stance is wavering.
In a recent interview with Morocco World News, Paulino Ros, a journalist and sociology professor in Murcia said that the photos “have no significance whatsoever, Spain’s stance has not changed and is in line with Morocco’s Sahara position.” Furthermore, Ros says that they were likely elevated as an attempted political maneuver on behalf of the Polisario Front.
As the Western Sahara dispute enters its sixth decade, Albares’s comments signal a diplomatic approach focused on dialogue, mutual understanding, and collaborative resolution.",success,MOROCCO,,MAR,,190,4,-10.0,0.462962962962969,25.0,-13.5,1,https://www.moroccoworldnews.com/2025/03/179846/spanish-fm-reiterates-support-for-morocco-calls-for-resolution-to-sahara-dispute/,www.moroccoworldnews.com,False,False,2025-03-05
1291311199,"My first brush with Mauritania came more than a decade ago, when I was doing the research for a new guidebook to Senegal for Bradt Guides. I was on top of the bastions of a whitewashed colonial-era fort in Senegal’s arid north, looking out over a wide bend in the blue-green Senegal River. Through the desert haze, the two acacia-studded riverbanks looked much the same, dun-coloured and busy with a hotchpotch of farmers, fishers, herders, traders and washerwomen going about their daily routine. But those on the far shore were actually across the border in Mauritania, a huge desert country I knew little about at the time.
I did know that it was suffering from a period of insecurity, triggered by a jihadist rebellion that made crossing the river unwise, despite the brisk back-and-forth of overloaded merchant pirogues taking place beneath me. The northerly aim of the old fort’s cannons also made it clear that the Mauritanian wildlands across the river were a source of considerable unease for the 19th-century French as well.
The country’s instability, particularly between 2005 and 2011, decimated its already small tourism sector. The world-famous Dakar Rally pulled up stakes and left the region in 2008, and the long-serving weekly tourist charter flights from Paris were soon cancelled as well.
So I, along with all the other Africa-bound tourists at the time, kept well away. Yet the mysterious lure of this expansive arid land remained. Four times the size of the UK and approaching 90 per cent pure desert, Mauritania straddles the transition zone between north and west Africa. Neither fully one nor the other, it sits geographically, historically, and culturally at the nexus of the two regions, and at the very heart of the Sahara.
What you need to know
- How do you get there? How do you get there? Fly to the capital Nouakchott or the Adrar regional capital of Atar. You’ll likely change planes in Paris, Casablanca or the Canary Islands. Point-Afrique (point-afrique.com/vols-atar) operates weekly charter flights from Paris to Atar between October and March for about £600
- Who will love it? Seriously adventurous travellers
- Best time to go Mauritania has three seasons: rainy, cool and hot. The main tourist season is from November to March when days are hot but not unbearable
Unlike its neighbours, Mauritania has successfully pushed back the jihadi threat, and in the process has opened its doors to tourism once again. The green area on the Foreign, Commonwealth & Development Office’s map of the country has been slowly but steadily expanding, the French charter flights are back (ASL Airlines from Paris and Marseille), and since 2024 nearly every attraction of note now lies within this officially approved zone.
Five years ago I finally got to experience the other side of the river, determined to put to rest some of the mysteries I’d been pondering since that day at the fort. By this time Mauritania’s Iron Ore Train had gone somewhat viral, and travellers from around the world were descending on this unlikely destination for an even more unlikely reason: to freight-hop on top of one of the world’s longest trains, which trundles over 400 miles daily between the inland mining town of Zouérate and the port in Mauritania’s second city of Nouadhibou. Riding its wagons of coal-black ore was Instagram catnip, though officially banned, and not what I was here for.
- Discover our full guide to adventure holidays
I rather wanted to see what it really meant for this unwieldy country, seemingly constructed from the scraps left behind after the 19th-century colonial carve-up of the African continent, to have its feet in two worlds. I wanted to meet its five million people, from the Pulaar-speaking farmers of the riverine south to the Arabophone camel herders of the desert north. And to visit perhaps Africa’s strangest capital, Nouakchott, which on the verge of independence was built from nothing.
The mountainous Adrar region
The mountainous Adrar region is the undisputed heart of Mauritanian tourism, home to spectacular plateaus, canyons and oases — plus the ancient stone-built entrepots of Chinguetti and Ouadane, marooned in the deepest desert and long since bypassed by changing trade routes. Though it’s fairly straightforward to travel around Mauritania on public transport, the Adrar is one place where joining a tour or hiring a guide pays real dividends. Here you can drop off the tarmac road for a solid week, following dry riverbeds and tracks across the dunes, visiting herders’ hamlets, lush palmeraies and oases so hidden you’ll wonder how anyone ever encountered them in the first place.
In the Adrar and all corners of the country I met a warm and hospitable people, who welcomed me unquestioningly and invited me to share in their traditions — starting with a relentless schedule of tea-drinking, served furiously hot and stupefyingly sweet. This green tea, known as atay, is a national obsession so profound it features on postage stamps, banknotes and traffic roundabouts. Alongside their Islamic faith, it is one of the handful of common elements that bring all Mauritanians together.
More of the highlights
Between rounds of tea I also managed to see some of the sights, and I was blown away by what I found. Sand dunes crashing into the ocean in the Banc d’Arguin National Park, crocodiles wallowing in secret mountain pools in Matmata, and families guarding libraries of centuries-old parchments in Chinguetti as if they were pure gold. Camel herders in sky-blue robes still plod from oasis to oasis, and the ruins of some of Africa’s great empires stand as silent witnesses to it all. I found a country where the rules of nomadic life still apply, and hospitality remains a sacred obligation. Dates were pressed into my hands and scarves wrapped around my head, all free of charge and with no hope of my ever repaying the favour.
From landing at Nouakchott’s airport, you can be camping under the Adrar’s desert stars or listening to the crash of waves in the Banc d’Arguin by nightfall, and in a circuit of just over a week you can take in the crumbling ancient cities of Chinguetti and Ouadane, the paradisiacal oases at Terjit and Mhaïreth, the staggering dunes of Azoueiga and the world’s second-largest monolith at Ben Amera — with a bit of time to spare.
To put it lightly, I was convinced — Mauritania was so much more than the industrial novelty of the train, or the wild YouTube conspiracies about the Richat Structure, a geological oddity in the Adrar, that some speculate once held the lost city of Atlantis (you can go check for yourself — it didn’t). And it was certainly more than a necessary stopover between Morocco and Senegal, as so many overlanders speeding down from Europe in their expedition vehicles seem to believe.
And so I found in Mauritania not only a raw and rewarding destination, but a success story that was ready to be told. So I set about pitching Bradt Guides a new guidebook to the country, and have returned multiple times in the process of researching that guide, travelling thousands of miles across this, the 28th-largest country in the world, by car, camel, sailboat and donkey cart — and yes, on the famous train too (surprisingly cold, unsurprisingly dusty).
It was therefore a pleasure, ten and a bit years after my first sight of the country, to stand on the other side of the river looking back at Senegal and its fort. It was then I understood that Mauritania was no longer a mystery, but a destination finally ready for its debut. The first English-language guidebook is in stores now, and may the world meet Mauritania with all the grace and hospitality it met me.
Mauritania by Sean Connolly (Bradt Travel £21.99). To order a copy go to timesbookshop.co.uk. Free UK standard P&P on orders over £25. Special discount available for Times+ members.
How to visit — the best tours of Mauritania
By Sean Connolly
A nine-day group tour of the highlights
Taking in the greatest hits of the Adrar region, Lupine Travel has a solid nine-day trip for anyone with time constraints who still wants to see as much of the country as possible. The itinerary takes in the towering Azoueiga dunes, legendary fortified cities at Chinguetti and Ouadane, and makes stops at both the mysterious Richat Structure and an otherworldly canyon oasis at Terjit. You then hop aboard the Iron Ore Train for the long, dusty haul back to the coast, before crossing into Western Sahara to see the Morocco-administered city of Dakhla and surrounds, which is where the tour concludes. You stay in guesthouses on the way.
Details Nine nights’ full board from £1,795pp, including transfers, transport, guide and excursions (lupinetravel.co.uk). Fly to Nouakchott
Sailing and the interior — 10 or 21 days
Native Eye Travel has two possible group trips, including a 10-day itinerary that starts out with a traditional sailing trip on the rugged Banc d’Arguin coast before cutting inland to the Ben Amera Monolith and the paradisiacal hidden oases of the Adrar desert. But if you just can’t get enough of Mauritania’s desert treasures, this tour operator has a unique 21-day adventure that is one of the finest adventures to be had anywhere in the Sahara. You’ll not only take in the country’s Unesco-recognised ksour, but also cross one of the Sahara’s largest sand seas en route to a series of truly remote desert outposts and archaeological finds. The accommodation is a mix of guesthouses and camping.  
Details Ten nights’ full board from £2,349pp, 21 nights’ full board from £6,799pp, including transfers, transport, guide and excursions (nativeeyetravel.com). Fly to Nouakchott
Remote Adrar and more
Untamed Borders has a 12-night itinerary that gets out into some of the furthest reaches of the mountainous Adrar region, including the little-visited forts and prehistoric rock art sites at El Beyyed and El Ghallaouiya, beyond which lie several hundred miles of unbroken dunescape. Make your way back towards the coast via the villages of Entekemkemt and Tanouchert, which have survived off the produce of the date palm for centuries. Finally, ride the world-famous Iron Ore Train for an overnight journey to the Atlantic. You’re camping and in guesthouses the rest of the time.
Details 12 nights’ full board from £1,835pp, including transfers, transport, guide and excursions (untamedborders.com/itinerary/mauritania). Fly to Nouakchott 
The Mauritanian government introduced an e-visa system in 2025. Applications are made online at anrpts.gov.mr/en/visa/requestvisa. After receiving the approval, present it in hard copy at the port of entry. The £48/€55 fee (for 30 days) is payable in cash only on arrival.
