1304514384,"“There is no B plan, no alternative, and this entails consequences, which I’m fully ready to shoulder.”
That’s Javier Bardem, in Cannes discussing his new film The Beloved, which was shot in the Western Sahara. Given the politics of that region and the controversy over Bardem’s recent statements about the conflict between Israel and the Palestinians, he was inevitably asked about whether he feared a backlash to his position, and whether the mood about those topics might be changing in Hollywood.
	“The fear does exist. Granted that one has to do things, even if you feel a bit scared or afraid
You have to be able to look at yourself in the mirror and look at yourself in the eyes, and that was my case. My mother taught me to be the way I am,” said the Oscar winner.
	
As for what might happen to him: “There is no B plan, no alternative, and this entails consequences, which I’m fully ready to shoulder. These consequences, well, I’ve heard about this, but as I said yesterday, I can’t corroborate things or provide facts or proofs. You have these denunciations.”
Watch on Deadline
Bardem then spoke about whether the tide is changing in Hollywood.
“I had a whole host of offers in the US, in Europe, in South America and in Spain, and that made me think that in narratives things are changing, and everyone is beginning to realize, thanks to the younger generation, which is more aware of situations, situations we’re experiencing quite directly on our phones and on other screens.”
And while the media can fall prey to both sides-ism, Bardem is unequivocal about one thing.
“Genocide is a fact. You can fight against it, you can try to justify it, explain it. That is a fact,” he said. “If you, if you, you can be against it, or you can justify it. If you justify it with your silence, or with your support, you are brought genocide. Those are facts for me. Now you can face that in different terms of statements. My statement is this one is the power that you all gave me. I don’t have any other power or more power than you guys, but this, and I use it in the best way I know.”
In terms of the conflict itself, Bardem added, “This is unacceptable. It cannot be justified, and there can be no reason, no explanation for this genocide. Therefore, I think that what is happening is quite the contrary. I believe that those who are drawing up the so-called blacklist will actually be exposed, and they are the ones who will be suffering consequences in terms of the public and on a social level, and this is a major change.”
I wonder about his silence on South Sudan and the much larger number of people killed. 🤷🏼♂️🤷🏼♂️🤷🏼♂️. Didn’t make his radar?
Most people charging genocide don’t know anything about the history and facts of this conflict. There is plenty to blame on both sides. However critically – Hamas knew exactly how Israel was going to react when they attacked on Oct 7th and then they went to hid in hundreds of miles of tunnels that had openings in nearly every building in Gaza and watched their people get slaughtered. Hamas’s goal is the return of Israel to Islam and nothing less. So Israel has an existential dilemma – they must eliminate Hamas. How would you suppose they should do it . After 2 years Hamas still refuses to disarm and they are still hiding in tunnels. If anyone is guilty fo genocide- its Hamas against its own people.
I love listening to people who play pretend for a living and know absolutely nothing about anything.
Obviously he meant the genocide in Nigeria and violence in Syria and of course the Islamic Republican Regime that murdered thousands. Right ? Clearly he discussed that.
1) whataboutism. 2) we don’t fund those. 3) those are abhorrent ALSO. 4) you name-checking them doesn’t discount the genocide also happening in Gaza.
You do realize that Israel could wipe out everyone in Gaza in an hour if they wanted to. But instead the run the most moral army in the world. You don’t have to believe me. Check with every major military in the western world and they will tell you the same.
Whataboutism.
I admire Bardem for his morality and for his bravery, since speaking up on this topic could be enormously detrimental to his career and his relationships in this industry.
To those trying to cancel him: you can try, but you know deep down that the tides are turning. Sixty-plus years of propaganda and indoctrination, when any criticism of Israel was shut down as antisemitism, is coming to an end.
To those just waking up to examine the issue, there’s lots of credentialed historians (including Israeli ones) you can read to get a clear handle of the situation. You will find that the truth is not really buried at all, and you can hear the objectives of this project clearly from the mouths of Israel’s founders and other famous politicians, and not just from the settler-Extreme Right today.
I started paying attention after a visit to Israel as a teenager in the eighties (ahead of the first Intifada) when I saw first hand how IDF soldiers hated and dehumanized ordinary Palestinians just going about their daily lives.
If you’re intellectually honest, you can do the same by traveling in East Jerusalem or the West Bank today. Or maybe just watch unedited clips on the internet free of commentary, and decide for yourself.
Or you can watch unedited clips on the internet, free of commentary, of the atrocities and genocide committed by Hamas on October 7, shot by the terrorists themselves, and decide for yourself.",success,WESTERN SAHARA,CANNES,MAR,FRA,193,4,-10.0,-4.42105263157895,25.0,-13.5,1,https://deadline.com/2026/05/javier-bardem-genocide-hollywood-blacklist-1236907880/,deadline.com,True,False,2026-05-17
