1316290068,"41 dead as Morocco-Ceuta migrant crisis spirals; Spain rushes troops, EU sounds alarm
A humanitarian crisis unfolded at Spain's North African enclave of Ceuta after at least 41 migrants died while attempting to cross from Morocco, prompting the Spanish government to deploy additional security forces as European leaders expressed concern over the escalating situation.
Juan Jesus Vivas, the regional president of Ceuta, said nearly 60,000 migrants had entered the Spanish enclave from Morocco within the past 24 hours, overwhelming local authorities and triggering violent clashes at the border.
Dozens killed during crossing attempt
According to Rachid Sbihi, head of a local workers' association representing Civil Guard officers, several migrants drowned while trying to swim across, while others died in a stampede near the breakwater fence at Tarajal beach, close to the Moroccan border checkpoint.
		
			🚨😳 WOAH: Spain says roughly 60,000 migrants crossed into Ceuta from Morocco in just 24 hours, an influx equal to about 70% of the city’s population.
			
			Authorities have deployed troops, at least 34 people died during the crossings, and officials call the situation… pic.twitter.com/b6mz4PeWij
The fatalities came as thousands of migrants attempted to enter the Spanish territory, marking one of the largest border breaches in recent years.
Sanchez condemns border breach
Spanish Prime Minister Pedro Sanchez travelled to Ceuta on Friday to assess the situation and condemned the mass crossing, describing it as ""a violation of Spain's territorial integrity.""
Blaming organised human trafficking networks for the tragedy, Sanchez said smugglers exploit vulnerable people with false promises.
""They deceive so many young people and ultimately lead many of them to their deaths—whether in the ocean or, as in this case, at Spain's border in the autonomous city of Ceuta,"" he said.
		
			Day 2 of the invasion of Spain has started.
			
			The illegal migrants are still pouring over from Morocco and crossing into Ceuta and Melilla by the thousands.
			
			More than 50 000 illegal migrants have broken through the border since yesterday. pic.twitter.com/BduFZguq5i
Spain steps up security
Spain's Interior Ministry said nearly half of those who crossed into Ceuta had already returned to Morocco.
The ministry also released footage showing migrants walking back through a border gate.
		
