To contain the crisis, Madrid has deployed additional troops, police personnel, divers, drones and patrol boats to reinforce security around the enclave.
Videos circulating on Thursday showed thousands of migrants entering Ceuta in a dramatic surge that caught authorities off guard.
Crisis sparks diplomatic tensions across europe
The unprecedented influx has triggered diplomatic concern across Europe, with several countries backing Spain while calling for stronger border controls.
	
European Commission President Ursula von der Leyen described the scenes in Ceuta as ""unacceptable"" and stressed that entry into the European Union must comply with established rules.
""We cannot allow anyone to come to our European Union without abiding by our rules,"" she said.
	
An EU official later said there was no indication that migrants had moved onward from Ceuta into mainland Europe.
Meanwhile, France tightened checks along its border with Spain, while Germany, Italy and Finland condemned the developments.
Italy also called for Spain's suspension from the Schengen free-travel area, a move supported by Finland and Denmark. France and the United Kingdom said they were prepared to assist Spain in managing the crisis.
Trump weighs in
The migrant surge also drew political reactions from the United States.
	
US President Donald Trump used the developments in Ceuta to criticise Democrats, warning that the United States could face a similar situation under a Democratic administration.
""It's terrible. Remember that picture. That's going to be us in three years if the wrong side gets in,"" Trump was quoted as saying. ""If the Democrats get in, you will not live a very good life.""
The crisis continues to test Spain's border management capabilities while raising fresh concerns over migration, security and cooperation between the European Union and Morocco.
