959939482,"Article by WN.Com Correspondent Dallas Darling
Despite genocide against the Sahrawi people of Western Sahara, the Trump administration just announced an agreement in which the US will recognize as legitimate the illegal occupation of that territory. The US also plans to sell 50 stealth F-35 fighter jets to the UAE, a country that has a history of human rights abuses. And Sudan is coming off the US terrorism blacklist. The reason Arab nations are suddenly achieving long-sought goals is America’s new foreign policy venture. It will recognize and establish trade and military pacts with any nation that normalizes ties with Israel, which continues to commit human rights abuses against Palestinians and take their lands.
Architect or Destroyer?
The proponent of the policy is President Donald Trump’s son-in-law, Jared Kushner. Kushner, a Senior Adviser, is an orthodox Jew and very sympathetic to the policies of Israel’s Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu. Said to be the architect of the administration’s Israel-Palestinian Peace Plan, he has never worked directly with Palestinian officials, choosing instead to ignore the main issue: Israel’s continued human rights violations and illegal settlements in Palestine, and the dream of a Palestinian state. Trump’s announcement-which came on Human Rights Day-means the US now officially recognizes the occupations of both Palestine and Western Sahara.
Who Remembers the Sahrawi Genocide?
In late 1975, Moroccan troops entered Western Sahara after Spanish forces withdrew from their former colony in what were literally the dying days of General Francisco Franco and his fascist regime.
Moroccan warplanes bombed the indigenous Sahrawi people with weapons including napalm and white phosphorous, killing thousands and fueling a mass exodus of the population into neighboring Algeria. 
Under King Hassan 11, the government orchestrated a “Green March” of hundreds of thousands of Moroccan civilians into Western Sahara to continue displacing the Sahrawi people and take over the fishery-rich coastline. (1) Critics likened it to genocide.
Human Rights Violations and the Kafala System
The United Nations and Amnesty International have condemned human rights violations in the UAE. In addition to subjecting detainees, including foreign nationals, to arbitrary arrest and detention, the UAE has a long history of torture and enforced disappearances. The authorities restrict free expression and religion and imprison government critics while holding them in dire conditions. Women continue to face discrimination. The UAE also maintains the Kafala System, which ties migrants to employers for decades and makes them vulnerable to labor abuses and exploitation. The nationality to thousands of individuals born within its borders is still denied. (2)
How to Get Away with State Sponsored Terrorism
Removing Sudan from the list of State Sponsored Terrorism might provide some humanitarian relief, but it may get worse since Sudan contributes to international terrorism. Of special concern are the number of training camps for ISIS and the Sudan People’s Liberation Army - including many others like the Armed Islamic group. The UN and Africa Watch have accused all of them of hostage taking and torture, indiscriminately attacking and killing civilians - including women, and aid workers, drafting children soldiers, and exporting toxic ideologies. The state’s officials and groups participating in terrorism also participate in an illegal and international arms market. (3)
A Greater Israel?
Kushner not only wants the Palestinians to strike a deal and forgo the settlement territories that Israel has illegally seized-and still seizes but envisions a Greater Israel. 
In a CNN interview he said: “If we don’t do this today, at the rate at which Israel is growing, I think that it (Israeli-Palestinian Peace) will never be able to be done. So we see this as the last chance for the Palestinians to have a state.” He added: “This is something that we inherited, the situation where Israel continues to grow and grow.” (4) Kushner, who was instrumental in moving the US Embassy from Tel Aviv to Jerusalem, also is behind Netanyahu’s proposed plan to annex 30 percent of the West Bank.
Dangerous Precedents
As the Trump administration’s policies towards an unequal peace agreement between Israel and Palestinians sparks more unrest and conflict, the short-sighted goals of arming the UAE, removing Sudan from the State Sponsored Terrorism list, and legitimizing the genocide and illegal occupation of Western Sahara, will further add to the instability in the region.
Indeed, the administration’s actions are bound to trigger more wars of aggression and acts of terrorism-which is already occurring with Israel’s illegal occupations of Palestinian territories. 
It also will set a precedent that the US is no longer an advocate of peace and democracy or human rights.
Birth of an Aggressor Nation
It goes without saying that the actions of the Trump administration and Kushner mirrors that of the UN and birth of an aggressor nation in 1948: Israel. The invocation of historical rights to Palestinian land was a matter of tactics and public relations, but in view of the Holocaust in Europe, Zionist justification appeared reasonable at the time. (5) This violation, at the expense of a Palestinian agreement, has haunted Palestinian and Israeli generations to this day-and the rest of the world. America’s new policies towards Morocco and Sudan and the selling of arms to the UAE in recognition of Israel will likely do the same as it triggers more suffering and more bloodshed.
Dallas Darling (darling@wn.com)
