981764758,"A top commander of the Sahrawi Arab Democratic Republic (SADR), Addah Al-Bendir was killed on 7th April near the ceasefire sand ridge border in western Sahara marking SADR-held territory. According to SADR, he was killed in an alleged airstrike by Moroccan defence forces. Though Morocco has stayed silent on the airstrike, the attack might be retaliation to the skirmishes along the ceasefire line for the past few months.
The Polisario is a rebel group of the Sahrawi people fighting for control over Western Sahara. The ceasefire was agreed upon in 1991 but last November, Polisario withdrew from the ceasefire agreement. Since then, irregular tensions have been witnessed along the ridge border between both parties.
The killing of a commander while on a patrol inside territories held by SADR is likely to escalate tensions with more ceasefire violations in the coming days. The Sahrawi violation of the ceasefire is not seriously intended to start any war with Morocco. It attracted international attention to the long running stalemate in the hopes of a permanent resolution. The SADR does not have the requisite military capacity to change the status quo in Western Sahara. It relies on Algeria for weapons and the current weapons used by Polisario is obsolete.
However Moroccan escalation of the conflict by using airpower does not bode well for regional peace. It is not likely to be receiving a welcome audience in Europe and the US which are its major partners. The United States under Trump administration accepted Morocco’s sovereignty over Western Africa and any serious escalation will force Washington to reconsider its decision. Neighbouring Algeria has maintained a neutral stance over this issue and not shown any inclination to support Polisario to escalate the situation.
Given that Polisario lacks any significant strength to take on morocco, it might intensify an indirect war using Sahrawi supporters in the West Saharan region held by Morocco. This is an actual serious threat as it will put serious constraints on security forces.
Hardliner Sahrawi groups and Islamic radicalization in Sahel area can become a breeding ground for training lone attack militants. Any such attack will be difficult to stop given limited capacity of Morocco in intelligence and reconnaissance. Suicide attacks on critical morocco assets can trigger a fast deterioration of peace and will push back any hopes of resumption of the ’91 ceasefire. Human Rights Violations in Western Sahara may compel US to take a harder stance on Morocco under Biden. US Secretary of State Blinken has already requested United Nations to appoint a Western Sahara representative, after the post became vacant in 2019. But increasing number of countries supporting Morocco’s claims on sovereignty may compel SADR to take extreme actions to gain attention.
If the conflict gets hot, Algeria is most likely to support Polisario with modern weapons, and even build up troops on its border with Morocco. If international attention does not gain traction on the issue, the extremists in Polisario group may try to increase militancy inside Morocco with guerrilla elements.
The best alternative to this issue would be to bury the escalation by starting a dialogue between both parties under the auspices of UN. The representative to Western Sahara must be at once appointed and given a mandate to halt any further deterioration. The most plausible solution to the conflict would be a return to status-quo i.e., ceasefire agreement.
The 1991 ceasefire agreement contained a provision for referendum but it has not been held over differences. The UN Western Sahara Diplomat must try to conclude the process of agreements over the content of Referendum. The referendum is the only effective way for Morocco to end the conflict with Polisario.
